WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.022 --> 00:00:01.980
ROHIT KAMATH: Hello, and
welcome to the session

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.130
on best practices for setting
up disaster recovery for SAP

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.780
systems in Google Cloud.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.318
My name is Rohit Kamath,
and in this video,

00:00:09.318 --> 00:00:10.860
I would like to tell
you a little bit

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.620
about the various options
you have for designing

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.710
and architecting a disaster
recovery solution for SAP

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.750
applications in Google Cloud.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.110
So let's get started.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.350
First, let's quickly
define disaster

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.220
as it relates to cloud.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.310
However unlikely
this event may be,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.740
where an entire geographic
region is considered

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.980
unavailable, meaning all
applications in this region

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.290
are also considered unavailable.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.300
This could be due to
a natural phenomenon

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.370
or an unnatural event.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.190
When such an event occurs, the
business continuity process

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.370
is triggered, where necessary
folks in the organization

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.560
are contacted and a
decision to failover

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.870
all business critical
applications to the disaster

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.030
recovery site is initiated.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.940
For this purpose, it is
always recommended to properly

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.000
test, plan and update
the business continuity

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.950
and disaster recovery plans.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.830
Needless to say, anytime
there's a change to the system

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.379
or the landscapes,
these plans will

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.270
have to be updated accordingly.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.880
Now let's take a
look at items to be

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.120
considered for best practices
to handle disaster recovery.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.270
To failover applications to
the disaster recovery site,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.350
proper setup, planning, testing
and validation are necessary.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.050
In every organization,
various applications

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.210
are necessary for
business to function.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.940
For each of these business
critical applications,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.670
certain key metrics, namely
recovery point objective

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.730
and recovery time objective,
need to be defined.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.860
Recovery point
objective is the amount

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.350
of data loss business
is willing to accept

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.090
in the event of a disaster.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.420
Meaning transactions that are
being recorded in the system

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.810
during normal business
operations on the primary site

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.960
that business is willing to live
without in case of a disaster.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.010
These very transactions will
then need to be either manually

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.970
or systematically re-entered
in the system on the DR site.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.260
Recovery time objective
on the other hand,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.320
is the amount of
time business is

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.420
willing to wait
for these systems

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.820
to be up and running
on the DR site.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.100
It also means it's
the amount of time

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.039
the teams have to prepare the
systems prior to handing them

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.790
over to the business.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.940
So it's the time taken
to restart your database,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.820
start up your
application servers,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.280
validate to ensure the
systems are up and running

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.520
in the right order, validate
connectivity of these systems

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.430
to peripheral systems,
validate business data,

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.640
and also ensure users
connectivity to the DR site.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.740
Depending on the RTO and RPO
defined for each application,

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.670
appropriate solutions can
be chosen and implemented.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.010
In case of a low
RTO and RPO, you

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.850
will need to ensure the systems
are able to failover quickly.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.400
And in case of
relaxed RTO and RPO,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.210
an appropriate backup restore
solution may be sufficient.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.870
To begin with, it is recommended
to reserve necessary instances

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.010
in the DR region to ensure
you have necessary compute

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.100
for you to stand up your
business critical applications.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.520
Second, also have a
proper business continuity

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.980
plan that is up to date.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.410
Test and validate
these plans at least

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.810
once a year, if not
more frequently.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.990
This ensures the teams involved
know exactly what needs

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.830
to be done in the event of a
DR. Depending on the application

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.530
and databases involved,
there are several ways

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.260
of replicating data and
corresponding application

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.530
configuration from primary
site to the DR site.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.790
As an example, if you're using
SAP HANA as the database,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.660
there is built-in replication,
referred to as HSR,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.420
or HANA System
Replication, available that

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.430
will help with this process.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:45.400
This HSR will not only
replicate data in the database,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.680
but will also help with database
configuration and other items.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.330
In case of other traditional
databases, like SAP ASE,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.570
log shipping mechanism can
be used to send data over

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.160
to the DR site.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.320
In case of application
servers, you

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.050
can use Google Cloud
Storage snapshot that

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.130
can help with this process.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.890
Now let's take a look
at some of the tools.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.560
If your landscape
is fairly simple

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.620
and only has a handful of
applications to failover,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.709
then you can adopt
native Google Cloud tools

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.779
to set up your DR process.

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:22.590
As an example, you can use
persistent disk snapshot

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.440
mechanism to create
snapshots and schedule

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.370
snapshots to ensure data is
backed up on a regular basis.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.010
And in the event you need
to recover from this,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.700
you can restore from these
snapshots onto the DR site.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.490
In case of larger
databases, you can

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.620
either continue to use
built-in database replication

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.360
and failover mechanism, or
save database backups to Google

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.490
Cloud Storage and
restore from them.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:52.980
These triggers and failovers
can be scripted in Google Cloud.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.870
In case of complex
landscapes, you

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.910
can use partner-provided
solutions

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.030
that are available in
Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.310
Apart from infrastructure,
you will also

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.800
need to ensure your network
is laid out appropriately.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.140
This is also to ensure users are
able to connect to the DR site.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.870
Typically, this is done
by updating your network

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.800
alias in the DNS to point
to new DR IP addresses.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.230
Second, you need to
ensure the primary

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.720
and DR sites are able
to talk to each other

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.120
during normal operations
to perform replication.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.410
This is all made simple
and easy in Google Cloud

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.740
via the dedicated high
bandwidth, low latency fiber,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.860
which allows connectivity
between sites without having

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.016
to go to the internet
or use an ISP.

00:05:38.016 --> 00:05:40.590
Virtual Private Cloud,
or VPC, provides

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.020
networking functionality
that can extend

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.090
your network around the globe.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.220
You can set up your
network in such a way

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.840
that your primary site and
DR sites are in the same VPC.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.650
Or you can also create a sub
network and IP address range

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.730
for the DR site.

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:00.960
Another option is also to
place your primary and DR

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.330
sites in separate VPCs,
or for that matter,

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.780
even in completely
different projects.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.420
That's the true flexibility
that Google Cloud provides

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.140
at the network layer, giving
you extensive control of how

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.540
you want to design
and lay your network.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.570
In closing, I would like to
say that disaster recovery

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.110
planning is very application
and organization-specific.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.740
Proper planning and testing
is paramount to having

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.760
a smooth failover in the
event of a DR. Google Cloud

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.900
can provide tools and integrate
with applications and databases

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.030
for a smooth failover process.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:40.500
Finally, I would like to
point you to documentation

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.020
that you can refer to
for items that I did not

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.100
cover today, and also find more
detailed documentation on rest

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.370
of the topics.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:48.870
Thank you.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

